			"text": "## Key:",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -240,
			"y": -2000,
			"width": 260,
			"height": 40
		},
		{
			"id": "9af065e7cf209e11",
			"styleAttributes": {},
			"text": ">## GENERATION\n\nThis is the stage where ideas are created. The divergent mode focuses on ideational fluency, usually, in this stage, ‘more is better' (Plucker et al 2019, p.45), because \"quality turns out to be (with surprisingly accurate predictive ability) a probabilistic consequence of quantity\" (Baer & Kaufman 2005, p.3). Simonton (2011) calls this _superfluity_, where the creator \"generates more ideas than are strictly necessary\". Moving beyond first ideas is also important, as philosopher Herbert Spencer said, \"early ideas are not usually true ideas.\" (in Osborn 1963, p.131) and \"distant associations and ideas found only after some time has passed tend to be, on average, the most original\" (Runco & Acar 2019, p.235). The convergent mode of this stage is focused on capturing and defining generated ideas. Deferment of judgement is important throughout (Osborn 1963, p.124)\n",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -390,
			"y": 1020,
			"width": 907,
			"height": 320,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "166c2977e7028a96",
			"styleAttributes": {},
			"text": ">## EVALUATION\n\nThe evaluation phase involves living with the idea, playing with it, testing and verifying, judging it. In this stage we create nothing new, but instead sift through our results, like panning for gold. You might \"develop a different relationship with your creation than you had while you were working on it\" (Fritz 1991, p.38). You alter your perspective to become the audience for your creation, enabling your to evaluate it and relate to to it \"by virtue of its own merits\" (ibid.). The imbrication of theory and practice that Nelson defines as praxis and vital to Practice-as-Research (PaR) is instantiated here, as Zembylas and Niederauer (2018, p.60) suggest: \"Reflective and conceptual activities [are] inseparable from physical activities\" and their monitoring is, to quote Shove et al (2012, p.100), \"part of, and not somehow outside, the enactments of practice\". \n",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -390,
			"y": 1700,
			"width": 907,
			"height": 340,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "5880cf6052555ea9",
			"styleAttributes": {
				"textAlign": "center"
			},
			"text": "# The Creativity Engine",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -130,
			"y": -95,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 80,
			"color": "1"
		},
		{
			"id": "4c48b7afb41a8697",
			"styleAttributes": {},
			"text": "#### EXAMPLES\n \n - _Associative Priming_\n\t - random words, quotes\n\t - images, sounds\n - _Affect Priming_\n\t - embodied cognition\n\t - system 1 vs. system 2\n - _Achievement Priming_\n\t - indicators of prior achievement, environment associated with achievement\n - _Ideation Priming_\n\t - ideational pools (see _Inspiration_ stage)",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -2900,
			"y": -80,
			"width": 700,
			"height": 320
		},
		{
			"id": "519cc16e4a462a3e",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "This should be in Associative. The 'random' part beneath it can stay. Perhaps group under title of 'Stochastic Resonance' or 'Serendipity'?",
			"x": 3860,
			"y": -1585,
			"width": 276,
			"height": 165,
			"color": "1"
		},
		{
			"id": "0a3d333e16b52df6",
			"styleAttributes": {},
			"text": "### OGDEN WORD WHEEL\n\nIn the smallest inner top circle are the words \"will,\" \"would,\" \"may\" and \"might;\" in the second ring are some verbs (\"put,\" \"take,\" \"get,\" \"give,\" \"keep,\" \"let,\" \"have,\" \"make,\" \"come,\" \"go\"); in the third are 12 adjectives like \"simple\" and \"some;\" in the fourth are 20 nouns, like \"sand\" and \"thing;\" in the fifth are 20 directions, like \"on\" and \"across;\" in the sixth are 21 nouns, like \"farm\" and \"edge;\" in the seventh are 10 adverbs and connectives, like \"here\" and \"but.\" By agreeing to put \"a\" or \"the\" where needed and starting the sentence with the word \"I,\" one can get many mechanically produced sentences as one turns each of the wheels independently of each of the others (p.99)\n\nif the elementary form of an action-sentence is \"Take one thing dash another thing,\" there are only 42 words in the set of all Basic English words that can by the wildest stretch of imagination fit where the dash is (Crovitz):\n\n- about across after against among and as \n  - at because before between but by down \n  - for from if in near not now \n  - of off on opposite or out over \n  - round still so then though through till \n  - to under up when where while with",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 4120,
			"y": 500,
			"width": 600,
			"height": 620,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "f70cb18b261a5745",
			"styleAttributes": {},
			"text": "### DEBONO THINKING HATS\n\n- _White_: objective, neutral, unbiased, facts\n- _Red_: emotional valure0laden, how thing affects you and others\n- _Black_: cautious, careful, considers the possible downsides, devil's advocate\n- _Yellow_: positive, upbeat, optimistic, best possibilities and outcomes\n- _Green_: creative expanding, growing, broadening\n- _Blue_: cool, unemotional, organising, categorising",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -5470,
			"y": 200,
			"width": 700,
			"height": 240,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "4c888fdc8551d9fc",
			"styleAttributes": {},
			"text": "### OGDEN WORD WHEEL\n\n- The elementary form of a statement of an action is \"taking one thing in some relation to another thing.\" The same elementary sentence is the form of a sentence to describe the statement of a discovery, an invention, or, indeed, an idea (p.99)\n\n- if the elementary form of an action-sentence is \"Take one thing dash another thing,\" there are only 42 words in the set of all Basic English words that can by the wildest stretch of imagination fit where the dash is. The words follow (from Crovitz, 1967).\n  - about across after against among and as \n  - at because before between but by down \n  - for from if in near not now \n  - of off on opposite or out over \n  - round still so then though through till \n  - to under up when where while with (pp.99-100)\n",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -6540,
			"y": 1360,
			"width": 1140,
			"height": 360,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "6d71d5f0f110132d",
			"styleAttributes": {},
			"text": "### QUESTIONS\n\n- _What would [blank] do?_ - How would [blank] solve this problem? A child, Carl Sagan, a clockwork soldier, a fiendish magpie, a friend, etc. (from Struthless video https://youtu.be/XJa7sE5Q1QA?si=BTkgkvsz4EarnPQr)",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -5360,
			"y": 1360,
			"width": 700,
			"height": 180,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "55b1bb9b0c187932",
			"styleAttributes": {
				"textAlign": "center"
			},
			"text": "Convention",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -615,
			"y": -520,
			"width": 205,
			"height": 50,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "bd0c859f4fe781ae",
